  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a data packet?",
      "a": "A packet is a small chunk of data, often around 1,500 bytes, with a header that says where it came from, where it is going and its place in the sequence. Big files are split into thousands of packets.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is data split into packets?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a router do?",
      "a": "A router connects networks together and forwards each packet towards its destination. Your home router links your devices to your internet provider and also usually gives you Wi-Fi.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a router for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a routing table?",
      "a": "A routing table is a router's list of destinations and the next router to send each one to. Routers build and update these tables by swapping information with their neighbours.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a router know where to send data?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if a router breaks?",
      "a": "Neighbouring routers notice it has stopped answering and update their routing tables, so packets flow around it on other paths. This is why the internet keeps working when parts fail.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the internet not break when one part fails?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an IP address?",
      "a": "An IP address is a number that identifies a device on a network, like a postal address for computers. The older IPv4 kind looks like four numbers separated by dots, such as 192.168.1.1.",
      "asks": [
        "What does IP stand for?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "An IPv4 address is 32 bits long, allowing about 4.3 billion addresses; IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long.",
    "Packets from the same file can travel by different routes and are reassembled in order at the destination."
  ],
